## Use Case for eu-ehds:TechnicalInteroperabilityAssessment and eu-ehds:TechnicalInteroperabilityProcesses
Description: This example demonstrates a unified lifecycle for interoperability governance within the EHDS. It begins with the implementation of 
a Cross-border Mapping Engine, categorised as a eu-ehds:TechnicalInteroperabilityProcesses, which serves as the functional infrastructure required 
to transform national medical records into the European Electronic Health Record Exchange Format. To ensure this data remains medically 
accurate and machine-readable across borders, the engine utilizes a specific dpv:TechnicalMeasure known as the SNOMED CT Clinical Terminology 
Engine. This terminology engine provides a single shared language that maps complex clinical concepts to scientifically validated international 
standards, satisfying the semantic interoperability requirements of EHDS Article 2(f). However, in a regulated health environment, having the 
technical capability is only the first step, as the system must also possess the legal authority to operate. This authority is captured through
the second concept, the EHDS Conformity Assessment, which is categorised as a eu-ehds:TechnicalInteroperabilityAssessment. This organisational 
measure represents the formal audit conducted by a Market Surveillance Authority (MSA) to grant the mandatory eu EHR Label required 
under EHDS Article 48. By utilizing the relationship dpv:isAssessmentOf, the example creates a machine-readable connection that links the legal 
audit to the technical mapping engine. This connection allows for automated compliance tracking where the validity of the technical process is 
dependent on the status of its assessment. If the audit status is marked as dpv:CompliancePassed and remains within its dpv:hasExpiryDate, the system 
is considered authorised for cross-border exchange. Otherwise, the link effectively breaks, signaling that the technical process is no longer legally 
cleared for use. 
